diff --git a/docs/csv_export.rst b/docs/csv_export.rst
index 34d81594b36e558f7bb0a8beb0c4cce7ad50beab..6e0fac8d4322cd17189e34f82136e07d34d7d076 100644
--- a/docs/csv_export.rst
+++ b/docs/csv_export.rst
@@ -39,12 +39,33 @@ The following options can be used to customize the CSVs returned by Datasette.
 ``?_header=off``
     This removes the first row of the CSV file specifying the headings - only the row data will be returned.
 
+``?_null=TEXT``
+    Use the specified text for SQL ``NULL`` values, for example ``?_null=NULL``.
+    By default, SQL ``NULL`` values are exported as empty fields. Omitting this
+    parameter or using an explicit empty value, ``?_null=``, preserves that
+    behavior. Empty strings, zero and other non-NULL values are unchanged.
+
+    This works for tables, views and custom SQL queries, including every page
+    streamed with ``_stream=1`` and exports with ``_header=off``. With
+    ``_labels=1``, a NULL foreign key uses the marker in both its value and
+    label columns. A SQL NULL label also uses the marker; a label that is an
+    empty string stays empty.
+
+    URL-encode the parameter value when constructing a URL. Unicode, commas,
+    double quotes and newlines in the decoded marker are handled by the usual
+    CSV quoting rules. For example, ``?_null=missing%2C%20%22value%22`` uses
+    ``missing, "value"`` as the marker. CSV debug output with ``_trace=1`` also
+    applies HTML escaping. This option does not change JSON row data.
+
 ``?_stream=on``
     Stream all matching records, not just the first page of results. See below.
 
 ``?_dl=on``
     Causes Datasette to return a ``content-disposition: attachment; filename="filename.csv"`` header.
 
+Binary values, including empty binary values, are exported as links to download
+the original bytes. A SQL NULL in a binary column uses the ``_null`` marker.
+
 Streaming all records
 ---------------------
 
